INT. VILLAGE HALL - FUNERAL RECEPTION - AFTERNOON

Rain on the tin roof. Grey light through tall windows. Trestle tables, sandwiches curling at the corners, an urn of tea gone lukewarm.

A WAITER circles with a tray of sherry. Two TEENAGERS in borrowed black hover by the biscuits. A spaniel sleeps under a table.

HARRIET PEMBERTON, 50s, pearls and a brittle smile, pours tea. Her brother GILES PEMBERTON, 50s, tweed and tired, takes a cup.

GILES
(accepting the tea, not drinking it)
Rather a good turnout, don't you think?

HARRIET
(straightening a doily)
Rather. Considering Dad didn't speak to half of them.

Outside, the rain thickens. A gutter gurgles.

GILES
(glancing at the spaniel)
Whose dog is that?

HARRIET
(shrugging)
The vicar's, I should think. Or nobody's.

A THIN WOMAN in a dark mac stands by the door, dripping, a bag clutched to her chest. She looks at the room as one looks at a station timetable.

GILES
(low, to Harriet)
Do you know her?

HARRIET
(not looking)
Wasn't she at the church? Back pew?

Thunder, distant, politely.

THE WOMAN crosses the room. She stops in front of Harriet.

THE WOMAN
I'm sorry to intrude. I did think about not coming.

HARRIET
(a small cold smile)
Not at all. Do have a sandwich.

THE WOMAN
(taking one, holding it)
That's kind.

A TEENAGER giggles. The other elbows him. The waiter passes with sherry; the woman declines.

NELL PEMBERTON, 20s, Harriet's daughter, arrives with an umbrella, shaking it dry.

NELL
(to Giles)
Uncle G, the car park's flooded. Mum, who's this?

HARRIET
(brightly)
We were just about to find out.

THE WOMAN
(setting the sandwich down)
My name is Margaret Dunne. Your father and I were together for nineteen years.

Silence. The urn ticks as it cools. The spaniel sighs.

GILES
(a little laugh that isn't one)
I think there's been some mistake.

HARRIET
(very calm)
Nineteen years. Dad was married to Mum for thirty-one.

MARGARET
(opening her bag)
Yes. That's rather the difficulty.

GILES
(standing very still)
Harriet.

HARRIET
(still pouring, tea overflowing the cup)
Giles.

^NELL
(stepping between them)
Stop it, both of you, you're being appalling.

GILES
(to Nell, gentle)
Darling, go and find your cousins.

NELL
(refusing)
No.

Margaret takes out an envelope and holds it out. Nobody takes it.

MARGARET
He asked me to give this to you, Harriet. After. Not before. He was very specific.

HARRIET
(finally setting the pot down)
That does sound like Dad.

She takes the envelope. Turns it over. Her thumb finds the seal.

HARRIET (CONT'D)
(quiet)
Giles, do be a love and see that Mrs Dunne has a proper cup of tea.

GILES
(beat)
Of course.

He pours. His hands shake. Rain on the roof, steady now.

HARRIET
(opening the envelope)
Nineteen years. And not one word.

She reads. Her face doesn't change. The teenagers have stopped giggling.

NELL
Mum?

HARRIET
(folding the page)
He's left her the house.

The spaniel lifts its head. The waiter, discreetly, withdraws with his tray.
